<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">1976 Tangshan earthquake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1976">1976</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Tangshan earthquake</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The 1976 Tangshan earthquake struck Tangshan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, early on 28 July 1976 local time, with a magnitude of Mw 7.6 and a maximum intensity of XI on the Mercalli scale. Its first shock began approximately 12 kilometres under the southern part of the city, on a fault running right under its centre. In minutes 85 percent of the buildings in Tangshan collapsed or were rendered unusable, all services failed and most highway and railway bridges collapsed or were seriously damaged. The official count stated 242,469 deaths, while historians accepted at least 300,000, making it the deadliest earthquake in recorded history excluding the famine deaths of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1556-shaanxi-earthquake">1556 Shaanxi earthquake</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Tangshan lies at the northern edge of the Beijing-Tianjin-Tangshan Plain, on soft sediments over strata holding large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> deposits, and the Tangshan fault runs right under the centre of the city. Seismic risk had been greatly underestimated, and almost all buildings were designed without seismic considerations, so that Tangshan was mainly a city of unreinforced brick buildings. The 1975 Haicheng earthquake, about 400 kilometres to the northeast, had killed far fewer people, an outcome credited to precautions taken after warnings and foreshocks. Tangshan had no perceptible foreshocks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The first shock was initially estimated at magnitude 8.1 and later recalculated to 7.6. The rupture extended 140 kilometres along the fault. In the zone of maximum intensity, about 10.5 kilometres long, nearly every building collapsed wholly or partly, and electricity, water and communications failed. Of 25 highway and railway bridges over the Douhe River in Tangshan, only six survived with minor damage. A second shock of magnitude 7.0 struck that afternoon near Luanxian, 70 kilometres to the east-northeast, and twelve aftershocks of magnitude 6 or more followed. Around 10,000 miners were underground, and most escaped within hours. Many residents at first mistook the earthquake for a Soviet nuclear attack.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The first public release of official figures came in November 1979. Historians cross-checked government files and found at least 300,000 deaths, noting that an internal report counted 17,599 people missing whom the official count never mentions. In the meantime high foreign estimates circulated, including 700,000 to 750,000 from an oceanographer. Mine production did not regain its earlier level until the end of 1977. The disaster came during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cultural-revolution">Cultural Revolution</a>, when belief in earthquake prediction had become part of ideological orthodoxy, and many seismologists consider the quake to have been unpredicted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mao-zedong">Mao Zedong</a> died six weeks later, on 9 September 1976.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1976 Tangshan earthquake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
